### How I’d verify it

I did not run the game. These are browser checks with expected results, not test results.

| Check | Expected result |
|---|---|
| Open the page and inspect DevTools | The road, start stripe, car, and trees render. No console errors. The clock stays at `0:00.000` until you accelerate. |
| Try both keyboard layouts | W and ↑ accelerate, S and ↓ brake to a stop, and both steering pairs move the car across the road. The shoulder slows the car. Arrow keys do not scroll the page. |
| Complete a lap | The best display changes from `None yet` at the first finish crossing. The current clock rolls over and keeps counting. It does not roll over while steering or stopping near the stripe. |
| Compare laps and reload | Brake during the second lap to make it slower. The best should stay unchanged. Run a faster lap and check that it replaces the best, then reload on the same origin to check persistence. |
| Restart and switch tabs | R resets the car and current clock but preserves the best. Switching tabs clears held keys and does not add hidden-tab time to the lap. |
| Check narrow and reduced-motion views | At 320 px wide and at 200% zoom, read the HUD and controls without clipping. With reduced motion enabled, the game uses a fixed overhead camera. |
| Block the CDN and reload | The HUD shows a startup error with a recovery hint instead of staying on the loading message. |

### Build decisions

- **Model the domain** led to track-relative driver state and a separate lap record instead of deriving laps from collisions with the painted stripe.
- **Prove it works** led to checks on the actual browser game, including the finish crossing and persistence across reloads. Runtime verification remains open.

**Throughput checkpoint.** Blocking first step: settle the track and timing model. Independent workstreams: n/a, one HTML deliverable. Shared mutable state: the simulation loop owns forward progress and lap updates. Smallest safe decomposition: one file with separate functions for scene setup, simulation, presentation, and storage.

Add a client-side export component and pass it the same rows and column definitions you use for the reports table. CSV needs no dependency, XLSX uses your existing `exceljs`, and PDF uses `jspdf` and `jspdf-autotable` for table layout and pagination.

Add the PDF dependencies with your package manager, for example:

```bash
npm install jspdf jspdf-autotable
```

### `ReportExport.tsx`

```tsx
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';

type Cell = string | number | boolean | Date | null | undefined;
type Format = 'csv' | 'xlsx' | 'pdf';

export type ExportColumn<Row> = {
  header: string;
  value: (row: Row) => Cell;
};

function cellText(value: Cell): string {
  if (value == null) return '';
  return value instanceof Date ? value.toISOString() : String(value);
}

function csvField(value: Cell): string {
  let text = cellText(value);

  // Quote fields for CSV syntax and neutralize spreadsheet formulas in text.
  // Actual numbers (including negative numbers) are left unchanged.
  if (
    typeof value === 'string' &&
    /^(?:\s*[=+\-@＝＋－＠]|[\t\r\n])/.test(text)
  ) {
    text = `'${text}`;
  }

  return `"${text.replace(/"/g, '""')}"`;
}

function download(blob: Blob, filename: string) {
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  const link = document.createElement('a');
  link.href = url;
  link.download = filename;

  document.body.appendChild(link);
  link.click();
  link.remove();

  // Give the browser time to start the download before releasing the URL.
  window.setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 30_000);
}

export default function ReportExport<Row>({
  rows,
  columns,
  filename = 'reports',
}: {
  rows: readonly Row[];
  columns: readonly ExportColumn<Row>[];
  filename?: string; // Base name without an extension
}) {
  const [busy, setBusy] = useState<Format | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  async function exportReport(format: Format) {
    setBusy(format);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const headers = columns.map((column) => column.header);
      const body = rows.map((row) =>
        columns.map((column) => column.value(row)),
      );

      switch (format) {
        case 'csv': {
          const csv = [headers, ...body]
            .map((row) => row.map(csvField).join(','))
            .join('\r\n');

          // The BOM helps Excel recognize UTF-8 when opening the CSV.
          download(
            new Blob(['\uFEFF', csv], { type: 'text/csv;charset=utf-8' }),
            `${filename}.csv`,
          );
          break;
        }

        case 'xlsx': {
          const { Workbook } = await import('exceljs');
          const workbook = new Workbook();
          const sheet = workbook.addWorksheet('Reports', {
            views: [{ state: 'frozen', ySplit: 1 }],
          });

          sheet.addRow(headers).font = { bold: true };

          for (const row of body) {
            const excelRow = sheet.addRow(row.map((value) => value ?? null));
            row.forEach((value, index) => {
              if (value instanceof Date) {
                excelRow.getCell(index + 1).numFmt = 'yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss';
              }
            });
          }

          headers.forEach((header, index) => {
            sheet.getColumn(index + 1).width = Math.min(
              40,
              Math.max(14, header.length + 4),
            );
          });

          const buffer = await workbook.xlsx.writeBuffer();
          // Copy into an ArrayBuffer for compatibility with DOM Blob typings.
          const bytes = new Uint8Array(buffer.byteLength);
          bytes.set(new Uint8Array(buffer));

          download(
            new Blob([bytes.buffer], {
              type: 'application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet',
            }),
            `${filename}.xlsx`,
          );
          break;
        }

        case 'pdf': {
          const [{ jsPDF }, { default: autoTable }] = await Promise.all([
            import('jspdf'),
            import('jspdf-autotable'),
          ]);

          const doc = new jsPDF({
            orientation: headers.length > 5 ? 'landscape' : 'portrait',
          });

          autoTable(doc, {
            head: [headers],
            body: body.map((row) => row.map(cellText)),
            showHead: 'everyPage',
            horizontalPageBreak: true,
            styles: { fontSize: 9, overflow: 'linebreak' },
          });

          download(doc.output('blob'), `${filename}.pdf`);
          break;
        }
      }
    } catch (cause) {
      console.error(`Report ${format} export failed`, cause);
      setError(`Could not export ${format.toUpperCase()}. Please try again.`);
    } finally {
      setBusy(null);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div role="group" aria-label="Export report" aria-busy={busy !== null}>
      {(['csv', 'xlsx', 'pdf'] as const).map((format) => (
        <button
          key={format}
          type="button"
          disabled={busy !== null || rows.length === 0 || columns.length === 0}
          onClick={() => void exportReport(format)}
        >
          {busy === format ? 'Exporting…' : `Export ${format.toUpperCase()}`}
        </button>
      ))}
      {error && <p role="alert">{error}</p>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Wire it into the reports page

Render it inside the client component that owns the table data. Define the exported columns explicitly so internal fields on the row objects don’t leak into the files.

```tsx
import ReportExport, { type ExportColumn } from './ReportExport';

// Adapt the accessors to your actual row type.
const exportColumns: ExportColumn<ReportRow>[] = [
  { header: 'Report', value: (row) => row.name },
  { header: 'Status', value: (row) => row.status },
  { header: 'Total', value: (row) => row.total },
];

// In the reports component, above the table:
<ReportExport
  rows={filteredAndSortedRows}
  columns={exportColumns}
  filename="reports"
/>
